## The idea

Values alone lose the shape, so also write a marker **"#" for every missing child**. Write the tree level by level with a queue; read it back with the same queue — each node in the queue takes the next two items as its children.

## Step by step

1. Serialize: BFS; write a value for a node, "#" for null; queue both children even when null.
2. Drop trailing "#"s.
3. Deserialize: first item is the root; for each node taken from the queue, the next two items are its left and right ("#" = none).

## Worked example

**Input:** tree [1, 2, 3, null, null, 4, 5]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| write | "1,2,3,#,#,4,5" |
| read 1 | children 2 and 3 |
| read 2 | children #, # → none |
| read 3 | children 4 and 5 |

**Result:** the same tree, rebuilt exactly

## Cost

O(n) time; the string is O(n). Iterative, so a 100,000-deep tree is fine.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// TreeNode is provided by the starter (Models.cs): int Value; TreeNode Left, Right.

// LeetCode 297 — turn a tree into a string and back. Level order, "#" for a missing child.
// Iterative both ways, so a very deep (one-sided) tree cannot overflow the stack.
public static class TreeCodec {
    public static string Serialize(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return "";
        var parts = new List<string>();
        var queue = new Queue<TreeNode>();
        queue.Enqueue(root);
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            var node = queue.Dequeue();
            if (node == null) { parts.Add("#"); continue; }
            parts.Add(node.Value.ToString());
            queue.Enqueue(node.Left);          // children are written even when missing ("#")
            queue.Enqueue(node.Right);
        }
        int end = parts.Count;                 // trailing "#"s carry no information
        while (end > 0 && parts[end - 1] == "#") end--;
        return string.Join(",", parts.GetRange(0, end));
    }

    public static TreeNode Deserialize(string data) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(data)) return null;
        string[] parts = data.Split(',');
        var root = new TreeNode(int.Parse(parts[0]));
        var queue = new Queue<TreeNode>();
        queue.Enqueue(root);
        int i = 1;
        while (queue.Count > 0 && i < parts.Length) {   // read children in the same order they were written
            var node = queue.Dequeue();
            if (i < parts.Length && parts[i] != "#") { node.Left = new TreeNode(int.Parse(parts[i])); queue.Enqueue(node.Left); }
            i++;
            if (i < parts.Length && parts[i] != "#") { node.Right = new TreeNode(int.Parse(parts[i])); queue.Enqueue(node.Right); }
            i++;
        }
        return root;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why not in-order?**

Many shapes share an in-order sequence. Pre-order or level order with null markers is unambiguous.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) time; the string is O(n).

**Q: Smaller output?**

Binary encoding (varints), or pre-order with a bitmap of which children exist.

**Q: Shared state?**

None — static functions.

**Q: A 1 GB tree.**

Stream the output to a file node by node; never build the whole string.

